  "compare": "exact",
  "referenceSolution": "def length_of_longest_substring(s: str) -> int:\n    last_seen = {}\n    start = best = 0\n    for i, ch in enumerate(s):\n        if ch in last_seen and last_seen[ch] >= start:\n            start = last_seen[ch] + 1\n        last_seen[ch] = i\n        best = max(best, i - start + 1)\n    return best\n",
  "targetComplexity": {"time": "O(n)", "space": "O(min(n, alphabet size))"},
  "hints": [
    "Think of a window over the string with no repeats inside it. When you add a new character, what makes the window invalid?",
    "Checking every substring for repeats costs O(n^2) or worse. Can the window slide forward without ever rescanning characters it already checked?",
    "When a repeat shows up, how far must the left edge move, and could it ever need to move backwards?",
    "Trace \"abba\" and \"tmmzuxt\". After the second 'b' (or 'm'), what happens when you later see an 'a' (or 't') whose previous position is now outside the window?",
    "A sliding window with a map from each character to its last index: jump the left edge past the previous occurrence, but only if that occurrence is inside the window."
  ],
